i'd disagree that it would make it significantly easier, since it does
little to actively help the player.  the only time it will really help
is before you hit an altar, and at that point you can't really affect
your prayer timeout terribly much anyway, so at best it's keeping
track of turns for you, which is something a player aware of prayer
timeout generally is able to gauge fairly easily anyway.  Then, you're
STILL tasked with even FINDING this special tool in a sea of spare
keys, lockpicks and mirrors before you hit an altar, and even then
they won't reach their full potential until after you find a useful
altar (or get super lucky).

after you hit an altar you have a better way of doing this, in that it
will both tell you whether or not you can pray, AND reduce timeout.

having the Priest class start out with them (uncursed, if the blessed
effect is still too much) fits with the game, and does that "class
differentiation" thing you're so adamant about :D (at least in the
early game)

Maybe in addition, to balance it even MORE so, maybe make it so
uncursed beads do nothing, cursed ones add to time-out, and blessed
ones tell you the current status with god and prayer.  Priests ONLY
can use uncursed beads as blessed beads (like an arc's touchstone).
this gives them the worst cursed effect of a trivial/non-magic tool,
with dubious benefit for "non-intended" classes (honestly, if you have
spare holy water to bless the beads, you probably have an altar
available to do their job ANYWAY).


